long double is clang's on this target, IEEE quad (113-bit mantissa, as on aarch64 and riscv64): bits/float.h said
x86-64's 80-bit format, so libm's long double functions (fabsl, frexpl, sqrtl, ...) took its bytes as that layout,
and WALI turned printf's and scanf's long double off (strtold and %Lf ended the program with SIGABRT). float.h is
aarch64's now. printf and scanf still work in double, as WALI had them, since long double arithmetic is software here
(printing and parsing doubles in it took two and a half times as long); a second build of each in long double
(floatscan_ld.c, vfprintf_ld.c, vfwprintf_ld.c) takes strtold, wcstold, scanf's %Lf and a format with an L in it.

